Let $\eta=2d-1$ and $$\label{eq:01-g-definition}
 g_X(z)=\frac{p_{N+1}(a_1,\ldots,a_N,z)}{p_N(a_1,\ldots,a_N)}.$$ Then $g_X$ is monic of degree $N$ for generic $X$, and $$\label{eq:01-arch-formula}
 m(X)=\sum_{i=1}^Na_i-\eta[z^{N-1}]g_X(z).$$ The right side is understood by continuation at removable singularities.

For fixed $a>0$ and every integer $n\ge1$, $$\label{eq:01-normalizer}
 Z_n(a)=\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}\Gamma(a+i)
             \prod_{0\le i<j<n}\frac{j-i}{2a+i+j}.$$ There exist constants $K_a,C_a$, depending only on $a$, such that $\mathbb P_{n,a}(r_n\le K_an)$ is bounded below by a positive constant independent of $n$, and, with $D_k=ak+k(k-1)/2$, $$\label{eq:01-small-coordinate}
 \mathbb P_{n,a}(r_k<h)
 \le\min\left\{1,\left(\frac{C_a h n^2}{k^3}\right)^{D_k}\right\},
 \qquad h>0,\quad 1\le k\le n.$$ In particular, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E_{n,1/2}\left(\sum_i r_i^{-1}\right)^{1/4}
       &\lesssim n^{1/2},\label{eq:01-reference-upper}\\
 \mathbb E_{n,2}\sum_i r_i^{-1}&\lesssim n^2,
       \label{eq:01-reference-inverse}\\
 \mathbb E_{n,1}r_1^{-1/4}&\gtrsim n^{1/2}.
       \label{eq:01-reference-lower}
\end{aligned}$$ The constants in the last three bounds are absolute.

